{"groups":[{"members":["c1","c2","c3"],"primary":"c2","title":"New Jersey Anti-Eviction Act: good cause required for eviction or lease non-renewal","requirement":"A residential landlord may not evict a tenant or refuse to renew a lease, written or oral, without one of the statutory good causes, and may recover possession only by tenant consent or a court judgment for possession; self-help evictions and lockouts are illegal. Except for nonpayment of rent, the cause must be stated in detail in a written Notice to Quit, and some grounds also require a prior Notice to Cease.","key_value":"Notice to Quit periods: 3 days (disorderly conduct, property damage, employment-based tenancy, drug offense, assault or terrorist threats, civil liability for theft, assault or drug activity, theft of property); 1 month (rule or lease breaches, nonpayment of a rent increase, habitual late payment, refusal of lease changes); 2 months (owner or buyer occupancy); 3 months (health and safety violations or removal from the market); 18 months (permanent retirement from residential use); 3 years (condominium, cooperative or fee-simple conversion). Nonpayment of rent requires no Notice to Quit, except a 14-day notice in federally subsidized housing. Relocation assistance for illegal occupancy: 6 times monthly rent. Owner-occupancy notice requires at least 6 months of occupancy. Retirement or demolition notice bars residential use for 5 years.","status":"in_force","effective_date":null,"end_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1 et seq.","coverage_conditions":"Most residential rental properties in New Jersey, including single-family homes, mobile homes and mobile home park land, apartment buildings and complexes, and rooming and boarding homes. Rooming and boarding houses also require at least 3 working days' notice to the County Welfare Board.","exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family premises with two or fewer rental units are excluded from the Act's good-cause list. Their tenants may still be removed only by court order for a narrower list of causes. Hotel or motel guests and guest houses rented to transient or seasonal guests are excluded, except hotel or motel occupants with no alternate residence who live there continually. A unit held in trust for a developmentally disabled immediate family member who permanently occupies it is excluded.","conflict_note":null},{"members":["c4"],"primary":"c4","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act (condominium/cooperative conversion)","requirement":"Eligible senior, disabled or disabled-veteran tenants in buildings being converted to a condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership may be protected from eviction for up to 40 years. The landlord or converter must notify all tenants of their right to file for protected tenancy, and applications are generally due within 60 days.","key_value":"40 years of protection from eviction; income limit the greater of 3x the county average per-person income or $50,000; 60-day application window","status":"in_force","effective_date":null,"end_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 to -61.39","coverage_conditions":"Buildings being converted to a condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership. Covers tenants who are at least 62 before the conversion recording date, permanently disabled, or honorably discharged veterans with at least a 60% service-connected disability. Tenants must have lived in the building at least one year before the conversion recording date and meet the income limit.","exemptions":null,"conflict_note":null}],"dropped":[]}
